{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794005587,"nonce":"QD5g9qeVBgryBkbbOrN5vhNmgk8=","message":"Hypothesis for the wasm half, from reading the code; not reproduced. In the fiber build, hlp_future_await parks the fiber (rt::park -\u003e fiber::park), and a parked main fiber is where the browser host unwinds the module back to JS (crates/ash_wasm_runtime/src/browser/fibers.rs), so promise callbacks, which complete the xgpu future, can run. hlp_sys_sleep goes to rt::sleep_for -\u003e ash::sleep_ns (std/src/rt.rs): with no fibers alive (fibers_active() false) it calls std::thread::sleep and never unwinds, so the page's event loop is starved for the whole sleep and an isReady poll never sees the future settle. If that is right, the fix is for Sys.sleep on a transformed module to park the logical thread on a timed waiter (as it already does when fibers are active) instead of sleeping the OS thread. Check first: ASH_FIBER_TRACE=1 on the polling program in a real page should show no park/unwind during the sleeps.","files":null}]}